<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Inductive Biases in Neural Networks on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/series/inductive-biases/</link><description>A from-scratch tour of why each neural architecture works, built as a pure-Python library you can read. Eight bite-sized posts, and a full monograph.</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 22:21:47 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/series/inductive-biases/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>A Network Computes Numbers. The Loss Decides What They Mean.</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-a-network-computes-numbers/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-a-network-computes-numbers/</guid><description>Function approximation, why one linear unit cannot learn XOR, and what a hidden layer actually buys. The opening of a from-scratch tour of inductive bias.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-a-network-computes-numbers/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>function-approximation</category><category>from-scratch</category></item><item><title>The Loss Function Is a Distribution Assumption</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-the-loss-is-a-distribution-assumption/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-the-loss-is-a-distribution-assumption/</guid><description>Choosing a loss is choosing a distribution for your output. Why every supervised network is a maximum-likelihood estimator, and why the gradient is always the residual.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-the-loss-is-a-distribution-assumption/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>maximum-likelihood</category><category>generalized-linear-models</category><category>loss-functions</category></item><item><title>What a Convolution Assumes</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-what-a-convolution-assumes/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-what-a-convolution-assumes/</guid><description>A convolution is a bet about images: nearby pixels matter together, and a feature detector should fire anywhere. How to test whether a model is actually using that bet.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-what-a-convolution-assumes/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>convolutional-networks</category><category>translation-equivariance</category><category>weight-sharing</category></item><item><title>Bengio&amp;#39;s Language Model: the Markov Assumption Made Architectural</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-bengios-language-model-markov-architectural/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-bengios-language-model-markov-architectural/</guid><description>The simplest neural language model: embed the last N tokens, concatenate, predict the next. What you give up with a fixed window, and what you gain by dropping recurrence.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-bengios-language-model-markov-architectural/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>language-models</category><category>embeddings</category></item><item><title>Recurrence Is Weight Sharing Across Time, and It Costs You</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-recurrence-is-weight-sharing-across-time/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-recurrence-is-weight-sharing-across-time/</guid><description>A recurrent network reuses one cell at every timestep and carries a state. That buys time-translation equivariance and unbounded reach in principle, and bills you in vanishing gradients.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-recurrence-is-weight-sharing-across-time/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>recurrent-networks</category><category>backpropagation-through-time</category><category>vanishing-gradients</category></item><item><title>Attention Is a Learned Pointer Dereference</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-attention-is-a-learned-pointer-dereference/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-attention-is-a-learned-pointer-dereference/</guid><description>An attention head is a learned content-addressable lookup: a query matches keys, retrieves a value, exactly like dereferencing a pointer. Depth is how many lookups you can compose.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-attention-is-a-learned-pointer-dereference/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>transformers</category><category>attention</category><category>content-addressable-memory</category></item><item><title>Attention Weight Is Not Information Flow</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-attention-weight-is-not-information-flow/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-attention-weight-is-not-information-flow/</guid><description>The trained pointer model reads exactly the right memory cell, provably. Its attention barely shows where. The gap, and the causal probe that closes it.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-attention-weight-is-not-information-flow/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>interpretability</category><category>induction-heads</category><category>in-context-learning</category></item><item><title>Reinforcement Learning Is Cross-Entropy, Reweighted by Reward</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-reinforcement-learning-is-cross-entropy-reweighted/</link><pubDate>Tue, 09 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-reinforcement-learning-is-cross-entropy-reweighted/</guid><description>When the only signal is a number at the end of a trajectory. How REINFORCE turns out to be the same gradient as classification, scaled by return, and where the theory tops out.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-09-reinforcement-learning-is-cross-entropy-reweighted/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Inductive Biases in Neural Networks</media:title></media:content><category>neural-networks</category><category>inductive-bias</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>policy-gradient</category><category>aixi</category></item></channel></rss>